window.__LIVE_NEWS__["2026-08-25"] = {"date":"2026-08-25","articles":[{"id":"n1787628896_0","date":"2026-08-25","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Iran faces 'greatest financial offensive ever', says US treasury secretary","summary":"Scott Bessent says the US will sever all economic ties with the country and that any nation partnering with Iran financially will also be isolated.","body":"BBC News Iran faces &#x27;greatest financial offensive ever&#x27;, says US treasury secretary Image source, Getty Images By Michael Race , Reporting from New York and Mitchell Labiak , Reporting from Business reporters Published 24 August 2026 Updated 3 hours ago The US announced a host of sanctions against Iran and threatened its supporters in what Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said was the single greatest financial offensive ever .\n\nLabelling it an economic D-Day for Iran, Bessent said the US would sever all economic ties with the country and added that any nation financially partnering with Iran would also be isolated.\n\nHowever, the latest threat to the Iranian regime follows several U-turns and extended deadlines from the White House in its efforts to end the conflict.\n\nIn response, Iran&#x27;s economy minister Ali Madanizadeh said there was a two-year plan to counter the sanctions, which would result in another defeat for Washington.\n\nThe war has led to hikes in global oil prices, and in response to the latest threat Iran warned it would shut down all oil exports from the region if the war continued\n\nThe Iranian regime has also issued a fresh warning to shipping to not pass through the Strait of Hormuz without its permission, according to Reuters.\n\nOne fifth of the world&#x27;s oil and gas usually passes through the strait, a narrow waterway south of Iran, but the flow has been effectively blocked by the country since the conflict began at the end of February, leading to higher oil prices globally.\n\nAt a press conference on Monday outlining what has been called Operation Economic Outcast , Bessent said the US was launching an economic onslaught against Iran&#x27;s financial connections around the globe .\n\nIran now faces a very clear choice with only two paths before them: complete global isolation....or a path back to normalcy with an opportunity to rejoin the global economy, he said.\n\nThe treasury secretary claimed America was no longer managing the Iranian threat, we are ending it .\n\nBessent said the Treasury Department had mapped networks, facilitators and financial channels used by Iran to evade sanctions to trade oil.\n\nThe department said it had issued determinations against five sectors: digital assets, technology, gold, aviation and shipping. The Treasury has also imposed sanctions on almost 60 entities, individuals and vessels.\n\nBessent said that such actions would tighten the noose and block every potential source of revenue for Iran&#x27;s Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps and the wider Iranian regime.\n\nIn a warning to governments and entities assisting or trading with Iran, he said they could not claim they are blind to enabling this activity .\n\nHe declined to highlight specific countries, but said Trump would be phoning world leaders with specific requests to cease their interactions with the regime .\n\nWhile he said it was important to give people time to understand the new sanctions, he added: They should know that we will move very quickly and that we are serious.\n\nMadanizadeh said Iran was fully prepared for the US sanctions .\n\nWe&#x27;ve been waiting for these plans for a long time, and the government is and was ready and has a two-year plan to manage these events, he told state television.\n\nHe also said neither China nor Russia had accepted the US measures, and predicted other countries would resist them.\n\nThe Chinese Foreign Ministry said sanctions and pressure tactics did not help and Beijing would do what was necessary to protect China&#x27;s interests.\n\nDavid Oxley, chief climate and commodities economist at Capital Economics, cast doubt on the effectiveness of the sanctions announcement.\n\nWith the renewed US naval blockade already strangling Iran&#x27;s oil exports, the direct impact of &#x27;economic D-Day&#x27; on Iran&#x27;s energy revenues will be somewhat of a damp squib, he said.\n\nWe suspect that the new package will have only a limited direct impact on Iranian energy flows in the short term.\n\nHe said that was partly due to the fact roughly 90% of Iran&#x27;s oil goes to China, a country which has not recognised US sanctions in the past and is unlikely to be cowed this time either .\n\nOver the course of the conflict so far, previous threats have included Trump saying in April that a whole civilisation will die tonight unless Iran agreed a deal to end the war and unblock the Strait of Hormuz.\n\nThe US eventually climbed down from that position after mediator Pakistan intervened and called for more diplomacy.\n\nThe economic impact of the Iran war is being felt in the US and across the world. Higher oil prices have fuelled concerns over the cost of living, with petrol and diesel prices much higher than they were a year ago.\n\nIn the US, gasoline prices have surpassed $4 a gallon and affordability is among the top concerns of American voters ahead of the mid-term elections in November.\n\nOn Monday, a barrel of Brent crude, the global benchmark for oil prices, was $92.\n\nLast week, Bessent announced the US government would intervene in the bond markets and buy back more government debt in a bid to boost demand for bonds and lower borrowing rates.\n\nBut the impact of the announcenment was short-lived, with long-term borrowing costs bouncing back up a day later.\n\nPublished 23 November 2021 How much could Trump&#x27;s &#x27;economic D-Day&#x27; hurt Iran?\n\nPublished 4 days ago The Iranian regime already faces tough economic sanctions from the US.\n\nFormer US President Barack Obama and several US allies had agreed a deal with the country in 2015 which lifted many sanctions in return for Iran agreeing to limit its nuclear programme.\n\nHowever, Trump pulled out of that deal in 2018, calling it defective at its core , and reimposed all US sanctions on Iran.\n\nDuring Joe Biden&#x27;s term as US president, he made some attempts to reinstate the Obama-era deal , but this did not happen.\n\nIn April this year, the Trump administration launched a wave of sanctions on foreign banks and firms doing business with Tehran after it became clear its military operations had not caused Iran&#x27;s regime to surrender.\n\nWatch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:27 Moment tornado tears through southern France. 00:00:27, play video Moment tornado tears through southern France 1:38 Inside the Taliban: an 18-year-old woman&#x27;s fight for divorce. 00:01:38, play video Inside the Taliban: an 18-year-old woman&#x27;s fight for divorce 0:46 Twelve arrested after crash that killed seven people. 00:00:46, play video Twelve arrested after crash that killed seven people 0:29 More downpours and thunderstorms are heading the UK&#x27;s way. 00:00:29, play video More downpours and thunderstorms are heading the UK&#x27;s way 0:39 Dog is rescued after two days trapped in badger tunnel. 00:00:39, play video Dog is rescued after two days trapped in badger tunnel 1:04 Burnham: UK there for Ukraine &#x27;as long as it takes&#x27; 00:01:04, play video Burnham: UK there for Ukraine &#x27;as long as it takes&#x27; 0:25 &#x27;Thanks to your country&#x27;: Zelensky greets PM during ceremony. 00:00:25, play video &#x27;Thanks to your country&#x27;: Zelensky greets PM during ceremony 0:50 Burnham is fourth UK PM to visit Kyiv in five years. 00:00:50, play video Burnham is fourth UK PM to visit Kyiv in five years 0:32 Climber told &#x27;there&#x27;s a queue&#x27; at mountain summit. 00:00:32, play video Climber told &#x27;there&#x27;s a queue&#x27; at mountain summit 0:59 How bad are the GTA 6 leaks? 00:00:59, play video How bad are the GTA 6 leaks? 1:14 &#x27;Doctors said I was &#x27;too young&#x27; to have endometriosis&#x27; 00:01:14, play video &#x27;Doctors said I was &#x27;too young&#x27; to have endometriosis&#x27; 0:46 Robot beats Usain Bolt&#x27;s 100m sprint record. 00:00:46, play video Robot beats Usain Bolt&#x27;s 100m sprint record 1:30 Do introverts make better leaders? 00:01:30, play video Do introverts make better leaders? 1:14 Why are Welsh women going to England for abortions? 00:01:14, play video Why are Welsh women going to England for abortions? 1:36 What triggers Trump to call reporters fake news? 00:01:36, play video What triggers Trump to call reporters fake news? 0:40 UK&#x27;s rarest breeding bird rears chicks in secret location. 00:00:40, play video UK&#x27;s rarest breeding bird rears chicks in secret location 1:20 Asha Bhosle: The most recorded artist in history. 00:01:20, play video Asha Bhosle: The most recorded artist in history 0:57 Maisie Adam thinks we should abolish names. 00:00:57, play video Maisie Adam thinks we should abolish names 0:43 Could extreme heat change school summer holidays? 00:00:43, play video Could extreme heat change school summer holidays? 1:05 &#x27;I&#x27;ve never prayed so much in such a short time&#x27; 00:01:05, play video &#x27;I&#x27;ve never prayed so much in such a short time&#x27; Top stories Organised crime arrests in A66 crash investigation\n\nHow the deadly A66 crash unfolded leaving seven dead\n\nBurnham vows support for Ukraine despite &#x27;outrageous threats&#x27; from Russia\n\nSir Chris Hoy: Campaigning for cancer screening should not be down to me\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Burnham hints at tax increases&#x27; and &#x27;Halt the hellrides&#x27;\n\nMoment tornado tears through southern France. Video Moment tornado tears through southern France\n\nBeyond China&#x27;s humanoid robots, a quieter machine revolution is unfolding\n\nThe grieving family who says the coroner&#x27;s courts aren&#x27;t working\n\n&#x27;What you see is what you pay&#x27; - why some US restaurants are banning tips\n\nAsbestos killed my mum in her 40s – was her school to blame?\n\nCharity? Acting? The Cotswolds? What will Harry and Meghan&#x27;s UK life look like?\n\nRoyal Watch: Get the latest royal stories and analysis with Sean Coughlan’s weekly newsletter\n\nBusby, Shankly and Stein: The makers of modern football\n\nThe rise and fall of a transatlantic cocaine empire\n\nFirst look: Explore Kenya&#x27;s spectacular birds of prey with Hamza\n\nA poignant story of an extraordinary blended family\n\n&#x27;Burnham hints at tax increases&#x27; and &#x27;Halt the hellrides&#x27;\n\nOrganised crime arrests in A66 crash investigation\n\nBeyond China&#x27;s humanoid robots, a quieter machine revolution is unfolding\n\n&#x27;What you see is what you pay&#x27; - why some US restaurants are banning tips\n\nLockerbie bombing trial postponed days before it was due to start\n\nMore than 70,000 social and affordable homes to be built across England as part of £39bn plan\n\nKit Harington joins HBO&#x27;s Harry Potter series as Gilderoy Lockhart\n\nIran faces &#x27;greatest financial offensive ever&#x27;, says US treasury secretary\n\nSurge in AI-assisted complaints putting extra strain on councils and schools\n\nBurnham vows support for Ukraine despite &#x27;outrageous threats&#x27; from Russia\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c0qxew81y83o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"administration","phonetic":"","definition":"政府；管理","example":"In April this year, the Trump administration launched a wave of sanctions on foreign banks and firms doing business with Tehran after it became clear its military operations had not caused Iran&#x27;s regime to surrender","example2":"The latest figures show a clear link between the economy and administration.","en_def":"a method of tending to or managing the affairs of a some group of people (especially the group's business affairs)；the persons (or committees or departments…"},{"word":"determination","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 决心, 果断\\ / [化] 判定; 测定","example":"The department said it had issued determinations against five sectors: digital assets, technology, gold, aviation and shipping.","example2":"The report highlighted determination as a key factor in the decision.","en_def":"the act of determining the properties of something, usually by research or calculation；the quality of being determined to do or achieve something; firmness o…"},{"word":"effectiveness","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 效力\\ /  有效性, 能行性","example":"David Oxley, chief climate and commodities economist at Capital Economics, cast doubt on the effectiveness of the sanctions announcement.","example2":"The report describes the effectiveness impact on local communities.","en_def":"power to be effective; the quality of being able to bring about an effect"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"被动语态 (Passive Voice)","explanation":"be 动词 + 过去分词。新闻大量使用被动语态，因为动作的承受者比执行者更重要。注意 by 短语引出真正的执行者，常被省略。","en":"The passive voice emphasizes the action or its receiver rather than the doer: be + past participle. Used heavily in news when the doer is unknown or less important. The agent can be added with 'by' or omitted entirely.","example":"Labelling it an economic D-Day for Iran, Bessent said the US would sever all economic ties with the country and added that any nation financially partnering with Iran would also be isolated.","example2":"The bridge was built in 1932 and is still used by thousands of commuters every day."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"cast doubt on","meaning":"对……提出质疑","en":"Usual usage in everyday/news English — \"The leak cast doubt on the official death toll.\"","example":"David Oxley, chief climate and commodities economist at Capital Economics, cast doubt on the effectiveness of the sanctions announcement.","example2":"The leak cast doubt on the official death toll."}]},{"id":"n1787628896_1","date":"2026-08-25","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"UK PM Burnham vows support for Ukraine despite 'outrageous threats' from Russia","summary":"It comes after Moscow warned of \"consequences\" over the reported use of British drones by Ukraine.","body":"BBC News Burnham vows support for Ukraine despite &#x27;outrageous threats&#x27; from Russia To play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser. This video can not be played\n\nIs UK&#x27;s Storm Shadow missile a game changer for Ukraine?\n\nAndy Burnham has promised to keep up the pressure on Russia despite Moscow&#x27;s outrageous threats , on his first official visit to Ukraine as UK prime minister.\n\nBurnham met Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky and chaired his first meeting of the coalition of the willing , set up by the UK and France under his predecessor Sir Keir Starmer.\n\nHe said the coalition was doing everything we can while urging the US Congress to ratchet up economic pressure on Russia.\n\nThe government has agreed to hand over blueprints for British-made components for the Scalp missile, a French version of the UK&#x27;s Storm Shadow cruise missile - allowing Ukraine to produce the long-range weapon domestically.\n\nUK missiles offer may help Ukraine - but they are not what Zelensky needs most\n\nPublished 14 hours ago Briton fighting for Russia killed in Ukraine\n\nPublished 11 hours ago Burnham said allies were doing everything we can to dig into our own stocks and help others dig into their stocks of air defences to protect Ukraine.\n\nThis is the moment to keep up the pressure on Russia, the moment to squeeze their ability to fund this illegal war, Burnham said, praising the strength of the Ukrainian people.\n\nUkraine has been Europe&#x27;s protector, not a drain on our security but the front line in keeping Europe safe.\n\nIt comes after Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov criticised the UK&#x27;s decision to share the missile blueprints. Peskov said it prevented even the slightest progress towards achieving peace.\n\nHe told reporters on Monday that the Russian military was already gathering data to locate any missile production sites to destroy them.\n\nBritain is involved in this war on the side of the Kyiv regime. Britain methodically and regularly adds fuel to the fire, he added.\n\nIn response, Burnham said: Who started the fire? That&#x27;s the question, isn&#x27;t it?\n\nHe said the issue of missile interceptors would be the main topic at the coalition of the willing meeting.\n\nSpeaking to BBC Newsnight, former Russian Deputy Foreign Minister Andrei Fedorov said the UK could face a semi-military response in retaliation for sharing military blueprints with Ukraine, but specified this would be not by Russia, but from unknown sources .\n\nSomething might happen with enterprises, with factories which are producing drones for Ukraine, Fedorov said.\n\nAsked if he was OK with a potential attack, he said: No, I don&#x27;t think it&#x27;s okay, because the problem is that we are now facing, in front of the danger, that Ukrainian conflict will be beyond Ukrainian borders.\n\nImage source, AFP via Getty Images Image caption, Burnham accepts an award from Volodymyr Zelensky on behalf of the British people recognising the UK&#x27;s support for Ukraine\n\nImage source, Reuters Image caption, Burnham chaired his first meeting of the coalition of the willing in Kyiv\n\nBurnham has been reaffirming the UK&#x27;s backing for Kyiv in the wake of escalating Russian strikes on Ukraine in recent weeks - including one on a shopping centre on Friday that killed at least 16 people.\n\nIn a joint statement after the meeting, Burnham and co-chairs French President Emmanuel Macron and German Chancellor Friedrich Merz said the group would aim to strength Kyiv&#x27;s air defences as a matter or urgent priority .\n\nThey also condemned Moscow&#x27;s systemic and escalating strikes against Ukraine&#x27;s cities, critical infrastructure and civilians and pledged to constrain Russia&#x27;s war chest through sanctions and actions against the shadow fleet .\n\nThe Coalition of the Willing is a partnership of 34 countries including Ukraine. Members met on Monday in either Kyiv or by video conference, with a release stating leaders once again reaffirmed their commitment to support Ukraine&#x27;s independence .\n\nIt came as the European Union said it had released €6.1bn (£5.2bn) in military aid for Ukraine to boost its air defences. The finance is part of a wider €90bn loan from the EU to Ukraine.\n\nThe UK and Ukraine have also announced a new military technology partnership , external giving British experts access to Kyiv&#x27;s Avengers AI Labs database.\n\nDowning Street says Ukraine has used sensors and cameras to capture data across the battlefield and it can be used to train AI models to solve key national security challenges faced by both countries.\n\nImage source, AFP via Getty Images Image caption, Burnham lays flowers at the graves of Ukrainian soldiers during a Monday service\n\nOn Monday morning the prime minister laid flowers at the graves of Ukrainian soldiers during a service at the National Military Memorial Cemetery.\n\nHe accepted an award from Zelensky on behalf of the British people recognising the UK&#x27;s support for Ukraine during an independence day event outside the St Sofia cathedral in Kyiv.\n\nIn a speech, Burnham compared Ukraine&#x27;s resistance to Russia to the UK&#x27;s fight with Nazi Germany, saying: My nation kept the flame of European freedom alive in the 20th century, you hold that flame in the 21st.\n\nIn a statement issued ahead of the visit, the prime minister said: On Ukraine&#x27;s 35th anniversary of independence, the Ukrainian people should be in no doubt: the UK is behind you today and for as long as it takes.\n\nI am proud of the contribution our country has made to Ukraine and I want the Ukrainian people to hear it directly from me that our support will not waver. After all, Ukraine&#x27;s security is our security.\n\nHe said the friendship Ukraine shared with the UK would long outlast Russia&#x27;s illegal war , adding: Russia should be in no doubt of our resolve. We will not back down until there is a just and lasting peace.\n\nMoscow earlier warned of consequences over the use of British drones by Ukraine in its recent ramping up of strikes on Russian infrastructure.\n\nKyiv has stepped up long-range attacks on Moscow and other Russian regions, targeting oil refineries and warehouses of the country&#x27;s largest online retailer, Wildberries.\n\nImage source, AFP via Getty Images The government is allowing UK defence company MBDA to declassify information on its components for the Scalp missile, as Ukraine and France aim to establish assembly lines in Ukraine.\n\nScalp missiles and its UK-produced version, the Storm Shadow, have already been provided to Kyiv by both Britain and France and used to hit military targets inside Russia.\n\nWhat are the Storm Shadow missiles Ukraine has fired into Russia?\n\nUkraine hopes it can also be given more Patriot interceptor missiles, with Zelensky again asking for more of the weapon, which are used to combat Russia&#x27;s ballistic missiles.\n\nUkraine has been relying on American supplies of the Patriots, but its stockpiles are running low due to the war in Iran, leaving Ukraine vulnerable to Russian attacks.\n\nThe Russian embassy in the UK has accused Britain of deliberately opting for an escalation of the Ukraine crisis , saying the deeper its involvement in the conflict... the higher the price it will pay .\n\nIn response, Burnham said last week: We are not fair-weather friends, we will be there in Ukraine&#x27;s hour of need and that won&#x27;t change.\n\nAsked on Tuesday about the Russian threat, Burnham maintained the UK was providing support so that Ukraine can defend itself and that&#x27;s been the British position all the way through this conflict, by previous prime ministers - it remains the case with me .\n\nThe visit comes as Russia confirmed its ambassador to the UK, Andrei Kelin, had left his post in London in recent weeks.\n\nThe Kremlin is yet to put forward a replacement but Russia&#x27;s embassy to the UK told the BBC it did not reflect an intention to downgrade diplomatic relations between the two nations.\n\nDeadly exchanges between Russia and Ukraine continued overnight, with two children killed and seven injured when Ukrainian drone debris hit a private children&#x27;s centre in Russia, local authorities say.\n\nA warehouse belonging to online retailer Ozon was also set on fire, two days after Ukraine said it would target the company for selling dual use items that could be used by the military.\n\nIt was at the third night in the row that Ozon&#x27;s warehouses had been targeted, with the company confirming on Saturday that a logistics centre in the Samara region had been hit and a warehouse in Orenburg on Sunday.\n\nThree people were also killed in a Russian attack on the north-eastern Ukrainian city of Kharkiv, regional Governor Oleh Syniehubov said.\n\nZelensky should be asked what he knew about government corruption, sacked minister tells BBC\n\nPublished 1 day ago Rescuers dig through Ukraine mall wreckage as Zelensky condemns &#x27;despicable&#x27; Russian strike\n\nPublished 2 days ago UK &#x27;supports Ukraine 100%&#x27;, Burnham says, after Russia&#x27;s drone warning\n\nPublished 6 days ago UK-made drones strike targets inside Russia\n\nPublished 16 August Get our flagship newsletter with all the headlines you need to start the day. Sign up here.\n\nWatch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:27 Moment tornado tears through southern France. 00:00:27, play video Moment tornado tears through southern France 1:38 Inside the Taliban: an 18-year-old woman&#x27;s fight for divorce. 00:01:38, play video Inside the Taliban: an 18-year-old woman&#x27;s fight for divorce 0:46 Twelve arrested after crash that killed seven people. 00:00:46, play video Twelve arrested after crash that killed seven people 0:29 More downpours and thunderstorms are heading the UK&#x27;s way. 00:00:29, play video More downpours and thunderstorms are heading the UK&#x27;s way 0:39 Dog is rescued after two days trapped in badger tunnel. 00:00:39, play video Dog is rescued after two days trapped in badger tunnel 1:04 Burnham: UK there for Ukraine &#x27;as long as it takes&#x27; 00:01:04, play video Burnham: UK there for Ukraine &#x27;as long as it takes&#x27; 0:25 &#x27;Thanks to your country&#x27;: Zelensky greets PM during ceremony. 00:00:25, play video &#x27;Thanks to your country&#x27;: Zelensky greets PM during ceremony 0:50 Burnham is fourth UK PM to visit Kyiv in five years. 00:00:50, play video Burnham is fourth UK PM to visit Kyiv in five years 0:32 Climber told &#x27;there&#x27;s a queue&#x27; at mountain summit. 00:00:32, play video Climber told &#x27;there&#x27;s a queue&#x27; at mountain summit 0:59 How bad are the GTA 6 leaks? 00:00:59, play video How bad are the GTA 6 leaks? 1:14 &#x27;Doctors said I was &#x27;too young&#x27; to have endometriosis&#x27; 00:01:14, play video &#x27;Doctors said I was &#x27;too young&#x27; to have endometriosis&#x27; 0:46 Robot beats Usain Bolt&#x27;s 100m sprint record. 00:00:46, play video Robot beats Usain Bolt&#x27;s 100m sprint record 1:30 Do introverts make better leaders? 00:01:30, play video Do introverts make better leaders? 1:14 Why are Welsh women going to England for abortions? 00:01:14, play video Why are Welsh women going to England for abortions? 1:36 What triggers Trump to call reporters fake news? 00:01:36, play video What triggers Trump to call reporters fake news? 0:40 UK&#x27;s rarest breeding bird rears chicks in secret location. 00:00:40, play video UK&#x27;s rarest breeding bird rears chicks in secret location 1:20 Asha Bhosle: The most recorded artist in history. 00:01:20, play video Asha Bhosle: The most recorded artist in history 0:57 Maisie Adam thinks we should abolish names. 00:00:57, play video Maisie Adam thinks we should abolish names 0:43 Could extreme heat change school summer holidays? 00:00:43, play video Could extreme heat change school summer holidays? 1:05 &#x27;I&#x27;ve never prayed so much in such a short time&#x27; 00:01:05, play video &#x27;I&#x27;ve never prayed so much in such a short time&#x27; Top stories Organised crime arrests in A66 crash investigation\n\nHow the deadly A66 crash unfolded leaving seven dead\n\nBurnham vows support for Ukraine despite &#x27;outrageous threats&#x27; from Russia\n\nSir Chris Hoy: Campaigning for cancer screening should not be down to me\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Burnham hints at tax increases&#x27; and &#x27;Halt the hellrides&#x27;\n\nMoment tornado tears through southern France. Video Moment tornado tears through southern France\n\nBeyond China&#x27;s humanoid robots, a quieter machine revolution is unfolding\n\nThe grieving family who says the coroner&#x27;s courts aren&#x27;t working\n\n&#x27;What you see is what you pay&#x27; - why some US restaurants are banning tips\n\nAsbestos killed my mum in her 40s – was her school to blame?\n\nCharity? Acting? The Cotswolds? What will Harry and Meghan&#x27;s UK life look like?\n\nRoyal Watch: Get the latest royal stories and analysis with Sean Coughlan’s weekly newsletter\n\nBusby, Shankly and Stein: The makers of modern football\n\nThe rise and fall of a transatlantic cocaine empire\n\nFirst look: Explore Kenya&#x27;s spectacular birds of prey with Hamza\n\nA poignant story of an extraordinary blended family\n\n&#x27;Burnham hints at tax increases&#x27; and &#x27;Halt the hellrides&#x27;\n\nOrganised crime arrests in A66 crash investigation\n\nBeyond China&#x27;s humanoid robots, a quieter machine revolution is unfolding\n\n&#x27;What you see is what you pay&#x27; - why some US restaurants are banning tips\n\nLockerbie bombing trial postponed days before it was due to start\n\nMore than 70,000 social and affordable homes to be built across England as part of £39bn plan\n\nKit Harington joins HBO&#x27;s Harry Potter series as Gilderoy Lockhart\n\nIran faces &#x27;greatest financial offensive ever&#x27;, says US treasury secretary\n\nSurge in AI-assisted complaints putting extra strain on councils and schools\n\nBurnham vows support for Ukraine despite &#x27;outrageous threats&#x27; from Russia\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/clyqypkrzq5o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"infrastructure","phonetic":"","definition":"基础设施","example":"They also condemned Moscow&#x27;s systemic and escalating strikes against Ukraine&#x27;s cities, critical infrastructure and civilians and pledged to constrain Russia&#x27;s war chest through sanctions and actions agains","example2":"The heavy rains badly damaged the region's infrastructure.","en_def":"the basic structure or features of a system or organization；the stock of basic facilities and capital equipment needed for the functioning of a country or area"},{"word":"investigation","phonetic":"/ɪnˌvestɪˈɡeɪʃn/","definition":"调查","example":"1:05 &#x27;I&#x27;ve never prayed so much in such a short time&#x27; 00:01:05, play video &#x27;I&#x27;ve never prayed so much in such a short time&#x27; Top stories Organised crime arrests in A66 crash investigation\n\nHo","_bank_example":"A BBC investigation found the accounts.","example2":"A full investigation into the crash is under way."},{"word":"extraordinary","phonetic":"","definition":"a. 非常的, 特别的, 非凡的\\ / [经] 非常的, 特别的, 临时的","example":"Royal Watch: Get the latest royal stories and analysis with Sean Coughlan’s weekly newsletter\n\nBusby, Shankly and Stein: The makers of modern football\n\nThe rise and fall of a transatlantic cocaine empire\n\nFirst look: Exp","example2":"The extraordinary situation has drawn widespread attention.","en_def":"a. beyond what is ordinary or usual; highly unusual or exceptional or remarkable；s. far more than usual or expected"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"间接引语 / 转述动词 (Reported Speech)","explanation":"said/told/warned/confirmed + that 从句（that 常省略）。新闻标题后正文常用转述动词引述当事人观点，注意从句语序不倒装。","en":"Reported (indirect) speech reports what someone said without using their exact words, typically with a reporting verb (said, told, added) and a tense shift: 'She said she was leaving.'","example":"He said the coalition was doing everything we can while urging the US Congress to ratchet up economic pressure on Russia.","example2":"She told reporters (that) the deal had already been signed."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"in the wake of","meaning":"在……之后（随之而来）","en":"following something, especially as a consequence e.g. \"Fuel prices surged in the wake of the conflict.\"","example":"Image source, AFP via Getty Images Image caption, Burnham accepts an award from Volodymyr Zelensky on behalf of the British people recognising the UK&#x27;s support for Ukraine\n\nImage source, Reuters Image caption, Burnh","example2":"Fuel prices surged in the wake of the conflict."}]},{"id":"n1787628896_2","date":"2026-08-25","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"US removes Syria from list of state sponsors of terrorism","summary":"The Trump administration has embraced Syria's new president, former al-Qaeda linked militant Ahmed al-Sharaa.","body":"BBC News US removes Syria from list of state sponsors of terrorism Image source, AP Image caption, US President Donald Trump welcomed his Syrian counterpart Ahmed al-Sharaa to the White House last year\n\nThe US has removed Syria from its list of state sponsors of terrorism after 47 years, lifting what it said was the final major barriers for private sector investment in the country and its reintegration into the global economy.\n\nUS Secretary Of State Marco Rubio said the move was in recognition of the Syrian government&#x27;s positive actions to fully distance itself from acts of international terrorism.\n\nThe Trump administration has embraced Syria&#x27;s new president, Ahmed al-Sharaa, a former militant who once had an American bounty on his head as the leader of an al-Qaeda off-shoot.\n\nHis Islamist group, Hayat Tahrir al-Sham (HTS), was also removed from the US list of global terrorist organisations.\n\nHTS, formerly known as al-Nusra Front, was al-Qaeda&#x27;s affiliate in Syria until Sharaa severed ties with the jihadist network in 2016.\n\nIn late 2024, HTS led the rebel offensive that overthrew former president Bashar al-Assad and it dominates Sharaa&#x27;s transitional administration.\n\nThe US hopes to bring Syria into its orbit rather than that of Russia or China. But the risks remain significant, with Syria still far from secure and lacking representative government. It is also at the centre of growing tensions between its neighbours Israel and Turkey.\n\nRubio said Monday&#x27;s move, which had been initially flagged in July, would give the Syrian a path to prosperity .\n\nUS Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said: Today&#x27;s action will help foster additional investment in Syria to promote political and economic stability.\n\nSyrian Foreign Minister Asaad Hassan al-Shaibani greeted the news as a historic step , according to state-run news agency SANA.\n\nWe will continue working to remove the effects of isolation and create an environment based on transparency, mutual interests and respect, he said.\n\nLast year, the US also revoked the designation of HTS as a Foreign Terrorist Organisation and removed Sharaa from its list of Specially Designated Global Terrorists.\n\nSyria&#x27;s removal from the US list of state sponsors of terrorism - where it had been since 1979 - leaves just three remaining: Cuba, North Korea and Iran.\n\nThe listing carries financial sanctions, restrictions on US foreign aid and defence exports and sales and can also penalise people and other countries that trade with the sanctioned state.\n\nSyria court sentences Assad-era grand mufti to life in prison\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:27 Moment tornado tears through southern France. 00:00:27, play video Moment tornado tears through southern France 1:38 Inside the Taliban: an 18-year-old woman&#x27;s fight for divorce. 00:01:38, play video Inside the Taliban: an 18-year-old woman&#x27;s fight for divorce 0:46 Twelve arrested after crash that killed seven people. 00:00:46, play video Twelve arrested after crash that killed seven people 0:29 More downpours and thunderstorms are heading the UK&#x27;s way. 00:00:29, play video More downpours and thunderstorms are heading the UK&#x27;s way 0:39 Dog is rescued after two days trapped in badger tunnel. 00:00:39, play video Dog is rescued after two days trapped in badger tunnel 1:04 Burnham: UK there for Ukraine &#x27;as long as it takes&#x27; 00:01:04, play video Burnham: UK there for Ukraine &#x27;as long as it takes&#x27; 0:25 &#x27;Thanks to your country&#x27;: Zelensky greets PM during ceremony. 00:00:25, play video &#x27;Thanks to your country&#x27;: Zelensky greets PM during ceremony 0:50 Burnham is fourth UK PM to visit Kyiv in five years. 00:00:50, play video Burnham is fourth UK PM to visit Kyiv in five years 0:32 Climber told &#x27;there&#x27;s a queue&#x27; at mountain summit. 00:00:32, play video Climber told &#x27;there&#x27;s a queue&#x27; at mountain summit 0:59 How bad are the GTA 6 leaks? 00:00:59, play video How bad are the GTA 6 leaks? 1:14 &#x27;Doctors said I was &#x27;too young&#x27; to have endometriosis&#x27; 00:01:14, play video &#x27;Doctors said I was &#x27;too young&#x27; to have endometriosis&#x27; 0:46 Robot beats Usain Bolt&#x27;s 100m sprint record. 00:00:46, play video Robot beats Usain Bolt&#x27;s 100m sprint record 1:30 Do introverts make better leaders? 00:01:30, play video Do introverts make better leaders? 1:14 Why are Welsh women going to England for abortions? 00:01:14, play video Why are Welsh women going to England for abortions? 1:36 What triggers Trump to call reporters fake news? 00:01:36, play video What triggers Trump to call reporters fake news? 0:40 UK&#x27;s rarest breeding bird rears chicks in secret location. 00:00:40, play video UK&#x27;s rarest breeding bird rears chicks in secret location 1:20 Asha Bhosle: The most recorded artist in history. 00:01:20, play video Asha Bhosle: The most recorded artist in history 0:57 Maisie Adam thinks we should abolish names. 00:00:57, play video Maisie Adam thinks we should abolish names 0:43 Could extreme heat change school summer holidays? 00:00:43, play video Could extreme heat change school summer holidays? 1:05 &#x27;I&#x27;ve never prayed so much in such a short time&#x27; 00:01:05, play video &#x27;I&#x27;ve never prayed so much in such a short time&#x27; Top stories Organised crime arrests in A66 crash investigation\n\nHow the deadly A66 crash unfolded leaving seven dead\n\nBurnham vows support for Ukraine despite &#x27;outrageous threats&#x27; from Russia\n\nSir Chris Hoy: Campaigning for cancer screening should not be down to me\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Burnham hints at tax increases&#x27; and &#x27;Halt the hellrides&#x27;\n\nMoment tornado tears through southern France. Video Moment tornado tears through southern France\n\nBeyond China&#x27;s humanoid robots, a quieter machine revolution is unfolding\n\nThe grieving family who says the coroner&#x27;s courts aren&#x27;t working\n\n&#x27;What you see is what you pay&#x27; - why some US restaurants are banning tips\n\nAsbestos killed my mum in her 40s – was her school to blame?\n\nCharity? Acting? The Cotswolds? What will Harry and Meghan&#x27;s UK life look like?\n\nRoyal Watch: Get the latest royal stories and analysis with Sean Coughlan’s weekly newsletter\n\nBusby, Shankly and Stein: The makers of modern football\n\nThe rise and fall of a transatlantic cocaine empire\n\nFirst look: Explore Kenya&#x27;s spectacular birds of prey with Hamza\n\nA poignant story of an extraordinary blended family\n\n&#x27;Burnham hints at tax increases&#x27; and &#x27;Halt the hellrides&#x27;\n\nOrganised crime arrests in A66 crash investigation\n\nBeyond China&#x27;s humanoid robots, a quieter machine revolution is unfolding\n\n&#x27;What you see is what you pay&#x27; - why some US restaurants are banning tips\n\nLockerbie bombing trial postponed days before it was due to start\n\nMore than 70,000 social and affordable homes to be built across England as part of £39bn plan\n\nKit Harington joins HBO&#x27;s Harry Potter series as Gilderoy Lockhart\n\nIran faces &#x27;greatest financial offensive ever&#x27;, says US treasury secretary\n\nSurge in AI-assisted complaints putting extra strain on councils and schools\n\nBurnham vows support for Ukraine despite &#x27;outrageous threats&#x27; from Russia\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c33knmyl0y5o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"international","phonetic":"","definition":"国际的","example":"US Secretary Of State Marco Rubio said the move was in recognition of the Syrian government&#x27;s positive actions to fully distance itself from acts of international terrorism.","example2":"The international situation has drawn widespread attention.","en_def":"any of several international socialist organizations；a. concerning or belonging to all or at least two or more nations"},{"word":"counterpart","phonetic":"","definition":"对等方","example":"BBC News US removes Syria from list of state sponsors of terrorism Image source, AP Image caption, US President Donald Trump welcomed his Syrian counterpart Ahmed al-Sharaa to the White House last year\n\nThe US has remove","example2":"Experts say counterpart will remain a central issue in the coming weeks.","en_def":"a person or thing having the same function or characteristics as another；a duplicate copy"},{"word":"environment","phonetic":"","definition":"环境","example":"We will continue working to remove the effects of isolation and create an environment based on transparency, mutual interests and respect, he said.","example2":"Campaigners welcomed the move, saying it reflects growing concern about environment.","en_def":"the totality of surrounding conditions；the area in which something exists or lives"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"定语从句 (Relative Clause)","explanation":"who/which/that 引导的定语从句修饰前面的名词。who 指人、which 指物，that 两者皆可；关系代词在从句中作宾语时可省略。","en":"A relative clause modifies a noun using who (people), which (things), or that (both). When the relative pronoun is the object it can be omitted: 'the law (that) the Senate passed'.","example":"The Trump administration has embraced Syria&#x27;s new president, Ahmed al-Sharaa, a former militant who once had an American bounty on his head as the leader of an al-Qaeda off-shoot.","example2":"The scientist who developed the vaccine won the Nobel Prize."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"opt for","meaning":"选择（opt for a compromise）","en":"\"opt for\" — a common English phrase; typical collocation: \"opt for a compromise\".","example":"","example2":"Faced with delays, many commuters opted for the train."}]},{"id":"n1787628896_3","date":"2026-08-25","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"French tourist dies in Death Valley after car gets stuck in mud","summary":"The 68-year-old man was found dead in California's salt flats amid 116°F (46.6C) heat.","body":"BBC News French tourist dies in Death Valley after car gets stuck in mud Image source, Anadolu via Getty Images Image caption, The 68-year-old was found dead around the salt flats of Death Valley\n\nA French tourist has died in California&#x27;s Death Valley after becoming stranded on the side of the road during 46.6C (116F) heat.\n\nPolice found 68-year-old Pierre Michel Formosa recently following a brief search, with the National Park service saying he died while trying to seek help in the blistering heat when his car got stuck in mud.\n\nThis is a heartbreaking reminder of how quickly conditions here can turn dangerous, Death Valley National Park Superintendent Mike Reynolds said in a statement.\n\nSoaring temperatures across the United States have caused millions of people to sweat through record heatwaves, leading to dozens of heat-related deaths.\n\nFormosa was driving through Death Valley with his French travelling companion last Monday when their vehicle became stuck in mud on West Side Road.\n\nThe pair sought help by walking across the salt flats, before severe heat prevented the 68-year-old from continuing after 1.3 miles, according to the Death Valley National Park.\n\nThe travelling companion managed to hail down a nearby driver, before rangers and police found Formosa later that afternoon where he was pronounced dead.\n\nDeath Valley is an extraordinary place, but its heat, distances, and remoteness can be dangerous, regardless of experience, Reynolds said.\n\nWe&#x27;re grateful to everyone who took part in the search, and our thoughts are with his loved ones.\n\nCoroners would determine the cause and manner of his death, the national park said.\n\nAn extreme heat warning is currently in effect across Death Valley, which brands itself as the hottest place on Earth , according to its official website, having recorded a global high of 57C (134F) in 1913.\n\nForecasters at the National Weather Service expected Southern California would experience its hottest week of summer so far , the bureau said in a recent statement.\n\nRecord heatwaves have endangered tourists visiting US landmarks throughout the summer.\n\nAt least four hikers died in June while trekking through the Grand Canyon , state authorities reported in Arizona.\n\nWatch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:27 Moment tornado tears through southern France. 00:00:27, play video Moment tornado tears through southern France 1:38 Inside the Taliban: an 18-year-old woman&#x27;s fight for divorce. 00:01:38, play video Inside the Taliban: an 18-year-old woman&#x27;s fight for divorce 0:46 Twelve arrested after crash that killed seven people. 00:00:46, play video Twelve arrested after crash that killed seven people 0:29 More downpours and thunderstorms are heading the UK&#x27;s way. 00:00:29, play video More downpours and thunderstorms are heading the UK&#x27;s way 0:39 Dog is rescued after two days trapped in badger tunnel. 00:00:39, play video Dog is rescued after two days trapped in badger tunnel 1:04 Burnham: UK there for Ukraine &#x27;as long as it takes&#x27; 00:01:04, play video Burnham: UK there for Ukraine &#x27;as long as it takes&#x27; 0:25 &#x27;Thanks to your country&#x27;: Zelensky greets PM during ceremony. 00:00:25, play video &#x27;Thanks to your country&#x27;: Zelensky greets PM during ceremony 0:50 Burnham is fourth UK PM to visit Kyiv in five years. 00:00:50, play video Burnham is fourth UK PM to visit Kyiv in five years 0:32 Climber told &#x27;there&#x27;s a queue&#x27; at mountain summit. 00:00:32, play video Climber told &#x27;there&#x27;s a queue&#x27; at mountain summit 0:59 How bad are the GTA 6 leaks? 00:00:59, play video How bad are the GTA 6 leaks? 1:14 &#x27;Doctors said I was &#x27;too young&#x27; to have endometriosis&#x27; 00:01:14, play video &#x27;Doctors said I was &#x27;too young&#x27; to have endometriosis&#x27; 0:46 Robot beats Usain Bolt&#x27;s 100m sprint record. 00:00:46, play video Robot beats Usain Bolt&#x27;s 100m sprint record 1:30 Do introverts make better leaders? 00:01:30, play video Do introverts make better leaders? 1:14 Why are Welsh women going to England for abortions? 00:01:14, play video Why are Welsh women going to England for abortions? 1:36 What triggers Trump to call reporters fake news? 00:01:36, play video What triggers Trump to call reporters fake news? 0:40 UK&#x27;s rarest breeding bird rears chicks in secret location. 00:00:40, play video UK&#x27;s rarest breeding bird rears chicks in secret location 1:20 Asha Bhosle: The most recorded artist in history. 00:01:20, play video Asha Bhosle: The most recorded artist in history 0:57 Maisie Adam thinks we should abolish names. 00:00:57, play video Maisie Adam thinks we should abolish names 0:43 Could extreme heat change school summer holidays? 00:00:43, play video Could extreme heat change school summer holidays? 1:05 &#x27;I&#x27;ve never prayed so much in such a short time&#x27; 00:01:05, play video &#x27;I&#x27;ve never prayed so much in such a short time&#x27; Top stories Organised crime arrests in A66 crash investigation\n\nHow the deadly A66 crash unfolded leaving seven dead\n\nBurnham vows support for Ukraine despite &#x27;outrageous threats&#x27; from Russia\n\nSir Chris Hoy: Campaigning for cancer screening should not be down to me\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Burnham hints at tax increases&#x27; and &#x27;Halt the hellrides&#x27;\n\nMoment tornado tears through southern France. Video Moment tornado tears through southern France\n\nBeyond China&#x27;s humanoid robots, a quieter machine revolution is unfolding\n\nThe grieving family who says the coroner&#x27;s courts aren&#x27;t working\n\n&#x27;What you see is what you pay&#x27; - why some US restaurants are banning tips\n\nAsbestos killed my mum in her 40s – was her school to blame?\n\nCharity? Acting? The Cotswolds? What will Harry and Meghan&#x27;s UK life look like?\n\nRoyal Watch: Get the latest royal stories and analysis with Sean Coughlan’s weekly newsletter\n\nBusby, Shankly and Stein: The makers of modern football\n\nThe rise and fall of a transatlantic cocaine empire\n\nFirst look: Explore Kenya&#x27;s spectacular birds of prey with Hamza\n\nA poignant story of an extraordinary blended family\n\n&#x27;Burnham hints at tax increases&#x27; and &#x27;Halt the hellrides&#x27;\n\nOrganised crime arrests in A66 crash investigation\n\nBeyond China&#x27;s humanoid robots, a quieter machine revolution is unfolding\n\nLockerbie bombing trial postponed days before it was due to start\n\n&#x27;What you see is what you pay&#x27; - why some US restaurants are banning tips\n\nMore than 70,000 social and affordable homes to be built across England as part of £39bn plan\n\nIran faces &#x27;greatest financial offensive ever&#x27;, says US treasury secretary\n\nKit Harington joins HBO&#x27;s Harry Potter series as Gilderoy Lockhart\n\nSurge in AI-assisted complaints putting extra strain on councils and schools\n\nBurnham vows support for Ukraine despite &#x27;outrageous threats&#x27; from Russia\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/clyl81jlzr2o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"temperature","phonetic":"","definition":"温度","example":"Soaring temperatures across the United States have caused millions of people to sweat through record heatwaves, leading to dozens of heat-related deaths.","example2":"Experts say temperature will remain a central issue in the coming weeks.","en_def":"the degree of hotness or coldness of a body or environment (corresponding to its molecular activity)；the somatic sensation of cold or heat"},{"word":"authorities","phonetic":"/ɔːˈθɒrətiz/","definition":"当局","example":"At least four hikers died in June while trekking through the Grand Canyon , state authorities reported in Arizona.","_bank_example":"Authorities urged calm.","example2":"The authorities have launched a full investigation into the incident.","en_def":"the people or organizations that have official power and responsibility"},{"word":"responsible","phonetic":"","definition":"负责的","example":"The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites.","example2":"The report describes the responsible impact on local communities.","en_def":"a. worthy of or requiring responsibility or trust; or held accountable；s. being the agent or cause"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"现在完成时 (Present Perfect)","explanation":"have/has + 过去分词。表示过去发生、对现在有影响的动作。新闻中常用来强调\"已经发生\"的结果，常与 already、just、so far 连用。","en":"The present perfect (have/has + past participle) links a past action to the present moment. Used when the exact time is not given or the result still matters now: 'Prices have fallen sharply.'","example":"BBC News French tourist dies in Death Valley after car gets stuck in mud Image source, Anadolu via Getty Images Image caption, The 68-year-old was found dead around the salt flats of Death Valley\n\nA French tourist has di","example2":"Prices have fallen sharply since January, and analysts expect the trend to continue."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"shore up","meaning":"支撑；扶持（shore up the economy）","en":"to support or strengthen something that is weak or failing e.g. \"Emergency funds were released to shore up the banking system.\"","example":"","example2":"Emergency funds were released to shore up the banking system."}]},{"id":"n1787628896_4","date":"2026-08-25","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Moment tornado tears through southern France","summary":"The tornado has injured more than 40 people and destroyed homes in villages.","body":"BBC News {\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"VideoObject\",\"name\":\"Moment tornado tears through southern France\",\"description\":\"The tornado has injured more than 40 people and destroyed homes in villages.\",\"thumbnailUrl\":[\"https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/standard/1920/galileo/p0p640vt.jpg\",\"https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/standard/1248/galileo/p0p640vt.jpg\",\"https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/standard/688/galileo/p0p640vt.jpg\",\"https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/standard/400/galileo/p0p640vt.jpg\"],\"uploadDate\":\"2026-08-25T00:16:35.408Z\",\"duration\":\"PT43S\"} Moment tornado tears through southern France To play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser. This video can not be played\n\nClose A destructive tornado has torn through villages in southern France, injuring more than 40 people.\n\nAn eyewitness from the village of Verzeille recorded the tornado forming beneath a thunderstorm. Winds of more than 100km/h (62mph) ripped the roofs off homes in Pomas.\n\nLocal authorities say about 2,800 households were still without electricity on Monday evening, while several roads were blocked.\n\nShare close panel Share page Copy link About sharing Related topics France Follow France\n\nclose panel You are now following France Updates from your News topics will appear in My News and in a collection on the News homepage . Tornadoes Follow Tornadoes\n\nclose panel You are now following Tornadoes Updates from your News topics will appear in My News and in a collection on the News homepage . Read description Explore more Moment tornado tears through southern France. Video, 00:00:43 Moment tornado tears through southern France\n\n0:43 Up Next. Tornadoes, severe weather hit US Midwest. Video, 00:01:11 Tornadoes, severe weather hit US Midwest\n\n1:11 Is UK&#x27;s Storm Shadow missile a game changer for Ukraine? Video, 00:01:37 Is UK&#x27;s Storm Shadow missile a game changer for Ukraine?\n\n1:37 Watch: Moment humanoid robot beats Usain Bolt&#x27;s 100m record. Video, 00:00:46 Watch: Moment humanoid robot beats Usain Bolt&#x27;s 100m record\n\n0:46 Editor&#x27;s recommendations Robot boxing, football and sprinting at World Humanoid Games. Video, 00:01:24 Robot boxing, football and sprinting at World Humanoid Games\n\n1:24 Watch: Taking a 180mph spin round America&#x27;s birthday Grand Prix racetrack. Video, 00:01:47 Watch: Taking a 180mph spin round America&#x27;s birthday Grand Prix racetrack\n\n1:47 Watch: How does El Niño affect world weather? Video, 00:01:00 Watch: How does El Niño affect world weather?\n\n1:00 Robot horse and rider steal the spotlight at Chinese conference. Video, 00:00:38 Robot horse and rider steal the spotlight at Chinese conference\n\n0:38 Watch: What does Harry&#x27;s return mean for his fractured relationship with William? Video, 00:01:08 Watch: What does Harry&#x27;s return mean for his fractured relationship with William?\n\n1:08 Watch: Fishermen rescued after five days adrift in cool box. Video, 00:00:27 Watch: Fishermen rescued after five days adrift in cool box\n\n0:27 Watch: What will happen to Harry and Meghan&#x27;s security arrangements? Video, 00:01:03 Watch: What will happen to Harry and Meghan&#x27;s security arrangements?\n\n1:03 Key moments from Harry and Meghan&#x27;s years since leaving UK. Video, 00:01:24 Key moments from Harry and Meghan&#x27;s years since leaving UK\n\n1:24 Multiple deadly Israeli strikes in Gaza in under 24 hours. Video, 00:01:11 Multiple deadly Israeli strikes in Gaza in under 24 hours\n\n1:11 Why is Ukraine&#x27;s ex-defence minister calling for wartime elections? Video, 00:01:02 Why is Ukraine&#x27;s ex-defence minister calling for wartime elections?\n\n1:02 Home News Sport Earth Reel Worklife Travel Culture Future Music TV Weather Sounds Terms of Use About the BBC Privacy Policy Cookies Accessibility Help Parental Guidance Contact the BBC Careers BBC emails for you Advertise with us Copyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/ckg8wkd0j7do?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"description","phonetic":"","definition":"描述","example":"BBC News {\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"VideoObject\",\"name\":\"Moment tornado tears through southern France\",\"description\":\"The tornado has injured more than 40 people and destroyed homes in villages.\",\"thumbnai","example2":"The report highlighted description as a key factor in the decision.","en_def":"a statement that represents something in words；the act of describing something"},{"word":"destructive","phonetic":"","definition":"a. 毁灭性的, 破坏的, 有害的\\ / [医] 破坏的, 毁坏的","example":"This video can not be played\n\nClose A destructive tornado has torn through villages in southern France, injuring more than 40 people.","example2":"The destructive blaze destroyed dozens of homes in minutes.","en_def":"a. causing destruction or much damage"},{"word":"electricity","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 电, 电流, 电学, 热情, 电力供应\\ / [化] 电学; 电","example":"Local authorities say about 2,800 households were still without electricity on Monday evening, while several roads were blocked.","example2":"The incident has once again put electricity under the spotlight.","en_def":"a physical phenomenon associated with stationary or moving electrons and protons；energy made available by the flow of electric charge through a conductor"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"将来表达 (Future: will / going to)","explanation":"will + 动词原形表预测或承诺；be going to 表计划好的事。新闻预测走势、宣布计划时高频出现。","en":"English marks the future with 'will' (spontaneous decisions, predictions) or 'going to' (plans, intentions). News uses 'will' for official decisions: 'The president will announce the plan.'","example":"Share close panel Share page Copy link About sharing Related topics France Follow France\n\nclose panel You are now following France Updates from your News topics will appear in My News and in a collection on the News home","example2":"Officials said the new regulation will take effect from March."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"draw on","meaning":"利用；借鉴（draw on experience）","en":"\"draw on\" — a common English phrase; typical collocation: \"draw on experience\".","example":"","example2":"The report draws on data from over 40 countries."}]}]};
